# Lesson 4, batch 2: two-way finesse decided by the danger hand. AJ74 opposite KT95 in hearts; if East gets in, a diamond through dummy's K5 costs a trick, so finesse into West. Here West has the queen: the finesse loses, safely.
title: Lose it to the safe hand
id: L04-23
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.6
student: S
board: 99
dealer: S
vulnerable: EW

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  8652   AJ74   K5     987
  E  43     32     JT982  J652
  S  AKQJ7  KT95   64     A3
  W  T9     Q86    AQ73   KQT4

auction:
  S 1S     = 13+ points, at least four spades
     not *: 17 points and five spades: open your long suit at the one level.
  W Pass
  N 2S     = 6-9 points, 3+ spades
  E Pass
  S 4S     = Game
     not *: You have 19 points counting your two doubletons, and partner has at least 6: enough for game.
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1♠ and partner raised to 2♠. With 19 points counting
  distribution, you bid game. You are declarer, because you bid spades
  first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 4
  losers: 4
  spades: 0 = Solid.
  hearts: 1 = The queen, unless you find it. You can finesse either way.
  diamonds: 2 = Two, unless West has the ace and dummy's king scores.
  clubs: 1 = Your small club.
  remind: You can afford three losers in 4♠. Count the obvious losers suit by suit in your own hand: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: Dummy's ♦K is safe if West leads diamonds or you lead toward it. Which opponent would you rather not have on lead?
play:
  1   W CK  N C7  E C2  S CA
      note: Four losers, one too many. Dummy's ♦K can score if West has the ace, as long as East never gets in to lead a diamond through it. Win the ♣A and draw trumps.
  2   S SA  W S9  N S2  E S3
  3   S SK  W ST  N S5  E S4
      note: Both opponents followed twice, so trumps are drawn.
  4   S S7  W C4  N S8  E C5
      note: Over to dummy with a spade, to lead hearts from there.
  5   N H4  E H3  S HT*  W HQ
      note: Lead a heart from dummy.
      HT key: Finesse the ten. If East has the queen, it wins. If West has it, West wins, but West can't hurt dummy's ♦K.
      HT not *: Did you think about which way to finesse? If you lose a heart to East, East leads a diamond through dummy's king, and you may lose two diamonds. Finesse the ten: if it loses, it loses to West.
  6   W CQ  N C8  E C6  S C3
      note: The finesse lost, but to the safe hand. West cashes the club loser.
  7   W H8  N HA  E H2  S H5
      note: West can't lead a diamond without giving dummy's king a trick.
  8   N HJ  E D2  S H9  W H6
  9   N H7  E D8  S HK  W CT
  10  S SQ  W D3  N S6  E D9
  11  S SJ  W D7  N C9  E DT
  12  S D4  W DQ  N DK  E DJ
      note: Lead a diamond toward dummy's king. West has the ace, so the king scores.
  13  N D5  E CJ  S D6  W DA
      note: 10 tricks: 4♠ made.

play recap:
  The plan: you counted four losers, one too many. Dummy's ♦K could score if
  West held the ace, but only if East never got in to lead a diamond through
  it.

  With AJ74 opposite KT95 you could finesse either way for the ♥Q. You chose
  the way that would lose, if at all, to West. It did lose, but West couldn't
  attack the king, and it won a trick at the end. Finesse the other way and
  lose to East, and a diamond through the king would have cost a trick: down
  one.
